    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is a faithful, additive mirror of the patterns already established in this codebase:\n\n- **`DisposableEmailValidator`** copies the exact shape of the sibling `PostcodeValidator` / `RegistrationDateValidator` \u2014 `final` class, private constructor, a single static `validate(...)`, a null/empty guard that keeps the request contract backward-compatible, and the same javadoc idiom. The validation logic belongs in a util validator, consistent with where this codebase keeps this kind of cross-cutting field check.\n- **`DisposableEmailException`** matches the other `Invalid*Exception` types (extends `RuntimeException`, builds its message in the constructor) and lives alongside them in `util`.\n- **`handleDisposableEmailException`** is structurally identical to `handleInvalidPostcodeException` / `handleInvalidTelephoneException` (same `@ExceptionHandler`/`@ResponseBody`, debug log, `BAD_REQUEST`, `detailBuild(... ERROR_INVALID_REQUEST)`), and is placed naturally among the peer 400 handlers. No catch-all is forming.\n- The controller call sites sit right next to the existing `PostcodeValidator.validate` / `RegistrationDateValidator.validate` calls. The `addOwner` path validates `owner.getEmail()` (already lower-cased via `toOwner`'s `normalizeEmail` mapping) while `updateOwner` validates `ownerMapper.normalizeEmail(...)` \u2014 this asymmetry is intentional and mirrors exactly how `telephone` is already handled on update (`normalizeTelephone(...)` called directly there), so it reads as idiomatic rather than inconsistent.\n- No parity gap with V2: `OwnerRestControllerV2` only exposes a paged read endpoint, so there is no create/update site there that should also be guarded.\n\nNothing is duplicated, logic is in the right unit, and a maintainer of this repo would find the addition natural.",
